she's been a pillar of the fight for  women's rights  and a liberal leader in the u.s now the  death of long-serving supreme court  justice ruth bader ginsburg  is poised to trigger a significant  political battle just weeks ahead of the  presidential election  even as the 87 year old was dying from  pancreatic cancer  she passed on a plea that there should  be no rush to fill the vacancy  in america's highest legal body al  jazeera's alan fisher  looks at ginsburg's legacy  they started to gather on the steps of  the supreme court in washington as news  of ruth bader ginsburg's death broke  they brought flowers and candles the  numbers swelled  people marking a loss they felt  personally  ruth bader ginsburg was physically small  but her presence loomed large on the  court  she was a blunt talking liberal who  often challenged her conservative  colleagues on the bench  born to jewish immigrants in brooklyn  new york in 1933  ginsburg was a brilliant student she was  one of only nine women to enroll in  harvard university law school in 1956  later transferring to columbia  university where she graduated at the  top of her class  liz ginsberg told one television  interviewer no law firms wanted to hire  a married mother  i had three strikes against me one i was  jewish  two i was a woman but the killer was i  was the mother of a four-year-old child  but as the 60s and 70s launched the  women's movement ginsburg found  a place in academia then as co-founder  of a women's rights project for the  american civil liberties union  she wrote the first supreme court brief  on gender discrimination  after 12 years as an appeals court judge  in washington d.c  president bill clinton appointed  ginsburg to the u.s supreme court in  1993.  ginsburg proved to be a consistent  liberal voice  on the increasingly conservative high  court she supported  abortion rights and gender equality she  also advocated using international laws  to shape u.s laws the outspoken ginsburg  fought several battles with cancer  her dying wish was the vacancy her  passing creates  will not be filled until after the  election  outside the court one trump supporter  with a megaphone urged the president to  move quickly  he was drowned out by the chance of  others the president has already  unveiled a list of people he wants to  put on the court if he wins a second  term  but leaving a rally he only praised  justice ginsburg  she led an amazing life what else can  you say she was an amazing woman  whether you agreed or not she was an  amazing woman  who led an  [Music]  in 2016 justice antonin scalia a  republican choice  passed before the presidential election  the senate refused to confirm a democrat  successor named by president obama  democratic candidate this time around  joe biden says there must be no decision  until the election has passed voters  should pick the president and the  president  should pick the justice for the senate  to consider  this was the position of the republican  senate took in  2016 when there were almost  10 months to go before the election  that's the position the united states  senate must take today  the republican leader in the senate  mitch mcconnell says he will press ahead  with nomination hearings  if given a name by donald trump that is  almost certainly going to happen  now he might not be able to get  confirmation hearings done by the  election in november  but could possibly push them through by  the inauguration in january  and if joe biden wins the election that  could create a constitutional crisis  here  in the united states ginsburg strived to  maintain strong liberal voices on the  supreme court to balance the  conservative ones  now her own voice has been silenced that  balance under threat  alan fisher al-jazeera outside the u.s  supreme court  tribute to coming in across the  political divide  chief justice john roberts announced  ginsburg death  in a statement he said that the u.s has  lost a justice of historic stature  and that the supreme court judges have  lost a cherished colleague  he went on to say that he's confident  future generations will remember  ginsburg  as a tireless and resolute champion of  justice  former u.s secretary of state hillary  clinton whose husband appointed ginsburg  to the supreme court  praised her work and said that there  will never be  another like her former u.s president  george w bush honored the 87 year old  saying that she dedicated much of her  life to the pursuit of justice  and equality and she inspired generation  generations of women and girls  well the makeup of the supreme court  could now take a sharp turn to the  political right  that's because president trump will  likely try to put a conservative  successor in place  even if he doesn't win november's  election the court is made up of nine  members  and up to now has been split 5-4 in the  conservatives favor  chief justice john roberts was appointed  by a republican president but recently  has acted as a swing vote this year  that's helped to secure narrow victories  for the liberals  on issues such as immigration and  abortion rights  appointing another conservative would  tip the balance further right  ahead of upcoming cases on same-sex  adoptions and health care  president trump has already named around  40 candidates to fill  a supreme court scott lucas  is co-founder of america unfiltered a  news analysis site focusing on u.s  politics and foreign policy he joins us  now  from birmingham in the uk scott your  thoughts  on the the death of justice ruth bader  ginsburg and  what it means for the supreme court and  for the country  well first and foremost that tribute to  ruth gator  uh vader ginsburg's legacy that legacy  of upholding and defending rights and  justice  uh as an attorney as a judge  and then as a justice not just on  women's rights although that should be  praised  but on widely civil rights rights in the  workplace  rights in education rights for the  impoverished  the question is what happens to that  legacy  in many ways we have seen this summer in  the united states with anti-racism  marches  those issues of rights and justice that  she's highlighted but what we'll see in  the next few weeks  is the attempt especially by the senate  majority leader mitch mcconnell  hypocritically given that he stood four  years ago for almost a year and blocked  an appointment by barack obama to the  court  he'll say we want to rush this  nomination through  and we know that not that donald trump  will try to  go for someone first and foremost as he  sees not only as a die-hard conservative  but someone that will defend him in the  white house  can they get that justice through by  november the third  because it's not just the prospect of  these important cases that you  highlighted  on abortion on adoption rights on donald  trump's attempt to gut  obamacare if we have a disputed election  with donald trump refusing to leave  office that case may eventually reach  the supreme court  now will there be an additional  conservative judge that will defend  trump  even if he refuses to accept a defeat to  joe biden on november the 3rd  as you say if um mitch mcconnell and the  republicans try to bulldoze of this  a new nomination through the senate  before the election  that would be pretty hypocritical will  that damage  the republican standing ahead of the  election  it's a gamble uh to be honest with you i  don't think mitch mcconnell cares mitch  mcconnell doesn't believe in consistency  i think he's gambling that there will be  enough of an appeal to red meet  republicans that this can galvanize them  to go to the polls on november 3rd not  just for the white house  but in that battle uh to re-elect  senators  because right now republican control is  on a knife edge  but you're quite right it could backfire  in two ways one is it could alienate  uh swing voters who see the hypocrisy  who have not yet decided  if they want to vote for donald trump or  joe biden or democrat or republican in  the senate  but secondly and specifically there are  several republican senators who are  facing  very difficult re-election battles susan  collins of maine for example  is one you're looking at lindsey graham  of south carolina who's the chair of the  senate judiciary committee  now will they support mcconnell's effort  to get a justice through by november the  3rd or given the effect on their  personal chances  will they hold back and thus prevent  mcconnell's political gain  and trying to get the hearings and  confirmation within the next six weeks  and what about president trump's  nominees  for uh the any vacancy on the uh  on the supreme court he's already  approved a number of uh of candidates  just how conservative are they what  difference would  uh con another conservative judge on the  supreme court  make to uh its behavior  they're conservative very very  conservative and really really  conservative there's a couple of  headline names there like senator ted  cruz  senator tom cotton i don't think that's  what you're going to get you're going to  get a judge from the lower ranks  of the federal system and it's not a  guarantee  it's not a guarantee that a trump  appointee will always be conservative  we've seen his first conservative neil  gorsuch vote with the moderates  on certain issues but i think on this  occasion donald trump will go for  someone who's a  real fire breather someone who will go  in and try to shred  for example health care rights that are  in obamacare  that will try to possibly shred economic  rights environmental protections  and possibly go for a supreme court  justice and this will be a big election  issue who will try to help overturn roe  versus wade  which was the key decision almost 50  years ago that guaranteed abortion  rights for women  always good to talk to you scott many  thanks indeed professor scott lucas  there  in birmingham thank you
 

